rotmgtradenode design doc
=====================

Two pieces: the NODE (rotmgtradenode, a self-hosted personal vault each player
runs on their own computer) and the HUB (rotmgtradehub, a website that lets
nodes find each other, swap items, and share a commons). The hub runs no
bots, never logs into the game, holds no items, no credentials and no money,
and sees vault contents only for vaults their owners chose to share (6.5).
Everything physical happens on players' own accounts from their own
machines. Both are open source. A third, private service that supplies
finished accounts to a node is designed separately and is not part of this
document.


1. Goals and non-goals
----------------------

Goals
  - every player can run their own vault from their own computer, as promised
    in the shutdown notice, with no dependency on a central fleet
  - the project cannot be taken down by one ban wave: no central roster, no
    central accounts, no central IP
  - nodes that opt in can trade with each other and pool items in a commons
  - a node owner can share their vault with friends through the hub, so a
    guest can deposit and withdraw without running the app
  - players who cannot make alts by hand can get them from a separate
    account service, which hands them to the node; the node walks the
    tutorial locally

Non-goals (for now)
  - no bots on the hub of any kind: no reserve accounts, no escrow, no /tell
    listener, no tutorial walkers, and no Realm traffic at all
  - no market and no points: no currency, no fractional decas, no credit or
    contribution ledger. Cross-node trades are item for item; the commons is
    free and capped
  - no raids, realm hunting, leaderboard, skin redeeming, or the dev API on the
    node


2. Ban-risk model (hard constraints)
------------------------------------

What the Sept 2026 wave showed: bots that only traded were ~2% banned. The
recycle lane (delete + recreate characters) and the scheduled mass-login lane
were what got the old cohort banned. So every node must obey:

  - a few accounts per player, logging in only
    through proxies the player pastes into the node (one account per exit IP
    at a time; the "proxy only" rule is on by default, so no proxies means
    no logins). A direct login from the home IP is an explicit opt-out
  - no character recycling, ever
  - no scheduled logins across accounts; bots log in when the owner uses them
  - the hub never logs into the game. The first game login of any account
    a node receives is its tutorial walk on that node, through the owner's
    own proxies
  - a Realm build change must not cause a mass kick: nodes check the current
    protocol before logging in (section 8)

Any future feature that breaks one of these needs its own risk write-up first.


3. Trust model
--------------

  - the hub is trusted by nodes. The node's source is public so players can
    see what runs on their machine and what it sends the hub
  - nodes are NOT trusted by the hub or by each other. A node runs on a player's
    machine and can lie about anything it reports
  - the only thing two nodes can prove to the hub is that they agree: a trade is
    real when both nodes sign the same receipt
  - a node is identified by a keypair it generates on first run. Logging in
    to the hub from the client links that key to a hub user account (4.3). The
    hub knows node public keys and never any credentials
  - a node verifies its own owner locally by /tell to the owner's own bot. The
    hub does not verify player IGNs; it records bot IGNs and lets counterparties
    attest them (see 6.2)


4. Components
-------------

4.1 Node (rotmgtradenode)
  - open source. Fork of rotmgcommunism `rewrite`, trimmed to: vault,
    deposit/withdraw, /tell owner login, inventory tracker, trade machine,
    backpack claim chore, tutorial walker (for hand-made and delivered accounts)
  - cherry-pick branch `rotmg-trade` (offers between vaults, item locking)
  - Electron app: tray shell around the existing Hono server + Vite SPA,
    auto-update, credentials in the OS keychain, SQLite under the user data dir.
    Windows first, code-signed; Linux and Mac builds if cheap. better-sqlite3
    needs an electron-rebuild step
  - opt-in ban telemetry (section 8)
  - works fully standalone (see 4.4). Hub connection is optional and off by
    default
  - roster: the owner's own alt accounts, added by hand or delivered by the
    separate account service (a pickup client, not built)
  - account storage (docs/relay/STORAGE.md): each account's vault chests,
    potion rack, Gift Chest and seasonal spoils chest are cold storage the
    owner moves items into and out of from the console; a run walks the
    account into the Vault and does the moves as INVSWAPs. Items in storage
    are not in the pool until taken out. The owner also picks which
    character an account logs in with. Characters trade with 8, 16 or 24
    slots (the upgraded backpack has 16)

4.2 Hub (rotmgtradehub)
  - open source. A website + API. No bots, no items, no credentials, no
    money, no Realm traffic. Every secret it uses is env, not code
  - holds: hub user accounts, node registry (public key, bot IGNs, version,
    last seen), offers, commons listings, trade receipts,
    vault-share grants
  - ban telemetry store (section 8)
  - hosting: a small site anywhere (Railway or a Vultr VPS)

4.3 Node modes and hub login
  Local mode (default, no hub account needed)
    - everything in 6.1 works: roster, vault, deposit/withdraw, /tell owner
      login, backpack chore, all state in the local SQLite
    - the app never contacts the hub except for the version check, which can
      be turned off
    - no hub features: no offers, no commons, no shared vaults

  Connected mode (log in to the hub from the client)
    - the client has a "Log in to rotmgtradehub" screen: hub email + password,
      or a Discord login if the hub offers one. This is a hub user account,
      separate from any game account
    - on first login the node sends its public key and the hub links it to the
      account. One hub account can have several nodes (desktop + laptop); each
      node is listed on the hub with its bot IGNs and last-seen time, and can be
      unlinked from the website
    - after linking, hub requests are signed with the node key, so the password
      is used once and never stored on the node; the hub issues a session
      bound to the node key that can be revoked from the website
    - logging in unlocks: posting and accepting offers, commons opt-in and
      withdraws, granting other hub users vault space on my node and using
      the space granted to me (6.5), and hub-side status (which of my nodes
      are online, open offers, commons items)
    - logging out or losing the hub does not touch local state. Open offers
      and commons listings for that node are hidden on the hub while the node
      is offline and resume when it reconnects. Locked items stay locked
      locally until the node hears the offer's final state
    - the roster and all credentials stay local in both modes; connected mode
      only adds a signed channel to the hub

4.4 Wire contract
  - node -> hub requests are signed with the node key, same shape as the
    existing HMAC-signed HttpSiteApi
  - the hub speaks the SiteApi-style pull model: a node asks "what should I do",
    the hub answers with an assignment


5. Data ownership
-----------------

Node SQLite (never leaves the machine)
  - accounts and passwords, roster, per-bot capacity
  - owned item instances (instance UUID, item id, enchant record, holder bot)
  - local vault ledger, offer state mirror

Hub database
  - hub user accounts (email, password hash or Discord id) and the nodes
    linked to each
  - nodes, bot IGN attestations
  - offers (give / want, status, expiry)
  - commons contributions and availability
  - vault shares: grants (owner node, guest user, IGN, slots per half, role,
    trade yes/no) and, for shared vaults only, each guest's published vault
    contents and the guest request queue
  - trade receipts
  - opt-in ban telemetry: per node, per account hash: suspended-at, last
    seen, last activity kind, node version, Realm build
  - current Realm build + protocol ids, for the node pre-login check

Item identity across nodes: instance UUIDs are minted per node tracker, so a
delivered item gets a new UUID on arrival. The hub matches items by item id +
enchant record, never by instance id.


6. Flows
--------

6.1 Owner deposit / withdraw (node only)
  - same as rotmgcommunism My Vault: owner logs in by /tell, claims, trades with
    their own bot. No hub involvement

6.2 P2P swap (node <-> node via hub)
  - a node posts an offer: give N items (locked, max 24 = 8 inventory + 16-slot
    backpack per trade window), want M items with wishlist-style filters.
    The items are the owner's, or a guest's from their personal vault on
    that node (6.5); a guest offer is tagged with the guest's hub account and
    shown under their name
  - another node accepts; the hub checks both sides have room and creates a
    rendezvous: server, both bot IGNs, both item lists, a time window
  - both nodes run the consolidation swap path (consolidate_give /
    consolidate_take with swapItems): each bot checks the other's offer
    against the assignment before accepting. The trade window is atomic, so
    no escrow is needed
  - larger swaps are chunked across windows; each completed window is its own
    receipt
  - each node signs a receipt (rendezvous id, window index, items seen leaving
    and arriving, TRADEDONE result) and sends it. The hub marks the window
    done only when both receipts match
  - each receipt also attests the counterparty's bot IGN as seen in the trade
    window; the hub counts attestations per IGN
  - abort rules: peer not online within the window, server usage gate red,
    trade window fails, receipts disagree -> offer returns to open, locked items
    unlock, both nodes told why. Disagreeing receipts freeze both nodes' open
    offers until reviewed
  - trade limits: a new node may have 1 open offer and small windows; limits
    grow with matching receipts

6.3 Commons (federated, no points)
  - a node opts in some of its items; contributed items stay on the
    contributor's bots and stay the contributor's until they are taken
  - the hub lists each item with live availability (contributor node seen
    within the last few minutes or not). Offline contributions are hidden,
    not deleted
  - anyone linked can take a listed item, free. There is no points system:
    contributions earn nothing and withdrawals cost nothing. What bounds it
    is a per-node daily cap the hub operator sets (default 8 hand-overs per
    rolling day), and that a node cannot take its own items
  - withdraw = a one-sided meeting: the contributor's bot gives, the
    withdrawer's bot receives, nothing comes back. Same receipt protocol as
    6.2: the hub records it only when the giver's "gave" equals the taker's
    "got" and the taker gave nothing. Taking an item unlists it at once; a
    failed, aborted or expired meeting lists it again and does not count
    against the cap
  - a contributor who pulls items back out of band simply stops listing them
    on the next publish; nothing is owed

6.4 Account supply
  - outside this document. The node's only part is a future pickup client
    that receives finished accounts and queues them for their tutorial
    walks (7)

6.5 Shared vaults via the hub
  - the owner grants a hub user access to one of their nodes. A grant is:
      slots       how many vault slots the guest gets, per half (seasonal /
                  non-seasonal): the guest's PERSONAL VAULT on the owner's
                  node. 0 in a half = nothing there
      role        deposit only            guest can put items in
                  withdraw own            guest can take back their own items
                  withdraw any            guest can take anything in the
                                          owner's pool too (a trusted friend)
                  co-owner                withdraw any + manage grants
      trade       yes / no: may the guest post and accept offers (6.2) with
                  the items in their personal vault
    Grants live on the hub, keyed by node. The owner sets, raises, lowers
    and revokes them from the Control panel; the node mirrors them
  - the owner grants to a hub user AND the IGN that user will trade from.
    The IGN is what the node enforces at trade time (below), so a guest
    account lying about its IGN cannot receive anything. Optionally the guest
    proves the IGN once by /tell to the owner's bot, which the node attests
    to the hub
  - the guest's personal vault is the node's existing per-user vault (users,
    vault_halves with a slot cap, vault_items owned by the user): items a
    guest deposits are theirs, sit on the owner's bots, and count against
    their slots. Lowering a quota below what is used blocks new deposits and
    incoming trades until the guest is under it again; it never evicts
  - while connected, a node with at least one grant publishes to the hub
    each guest's vault (item id, enchant record, holder bot online or not,
    slots used / granted). Visible only to the owner and that guest. Nodes
    with no grants publish nothing
  - the guest uses the hub website, or their own client logged in to the hub,
    to browse their vault and queue a deposit or a withdraw. They do not need
    to run the app or own any accounts
  - the hub turns the request into an assignment (guest IGN, server, items,
    vault user) and the owner's node pulls it like any other: its bot goes to
    the server and trades with that IGN only. Same machinery as the old site's
    personal-vault deposit/withdraw and as a commons withdraw (6.3)
  - guest trading (trade = yes): the guest posts and accepts offers on the
    hub exactly like a node owner, but the offer is tagged "on behalf of"
    the guest's hub account and may give only items from the guest's own
    vault. What the guest receives lands in their vault, so an accept is
    refused when it would exceed their slots (items held + wishes pending +
    incoming). The node's bot still does the physical trade; the node signs
    the receipt; limits, attestations and freezes stay per NODE, so an owner
    is on the hook for their guests and picks them accordingly
  - no receipt protocol between guest and owner: the guest trusts the owner
    by accepting the grant, and the owner runs the node, so the node's own
    report closes the request
  - offline node: the vault shows as offline, queued requests wait until the
    node reconnects and expire after a set time. Offers the guest posted are
    hidden while the node is offline, like the owner's
  - the owner's client shows every grant with slots used / granted, pending
    guest requests, and can pause sharing or a single guest's trading
  - local mode has no shared vaults. A node-local /tell allowlist could be
    added later for people who never log in to the hub; not in scope now
  - a node with many guests is a mini pool. The ban-risk rules in 2 still
    bound it: a few accounts per exit IP however many people use them, so
    the sum of quotas is bounded by what the roster can hold

7. Node scope: keep / cut from rotmgcommunism
---------------------------------------------

Keep: GameClient, protocol codecs, inventory tracker, trade machine,
per-bot capacity, chunked withdraw, /tell login, My Vault UI, backpack claim
chore (manual, per account), the tutorial walker (walks hand-made and freshly
delivered accounts), the account pickup client, Realm server list refresh
at boot.

Nothing from the old account generator's registration code stays on the node.

Cut: backpack scheduler lanes (recycle, daily login, audit), raids, realm
hunting, pool leaderboard, skin redeem, dev API, ban sweep,
server usage reporting to a site (replaced by a node-local check).


8. Versioning, protocol safety, telemetry
-----------------------------------------

Versioning
  - the hub publishes a minimum node version; older nodes get read-only hub
    access until updated
  - Realm build changes (protocol.json from the him.is feed) ship as node
    updates through Electron auto-update
  - a node must keep working with the hub down: all hub calls fail soft

Protocol-break safety
  - a Realm patch that changes packet ids kicks every bot that logs in with
    stale codecs, and a synchronised kick across every node is a ban signal
  - before any game login the node compares its built-in Realm build id and
    protocol hash against the current one. Source, in order: the hub's
    current-build endpoint, the him.is protocol feed, and as a last resort
    the build id reported by Realm's own app-init/version endpoint
  - mismatch = the node refuses to log in, tells the owner "Realm updated,
    waiting for a rotmgtradenode update", and keeps checking for the auto-update.
    Local mode does the same using the feed directly
  - the hub flips its current build as soon as the operator confirms the new
    protocol, and the node release that carries it goes out through
    auto-update

Server list refresh
  - the node fetches Realm's server list at boot and on every login gate
    rather than shipping a static IP table, so a server move never strands a
    node

Opt-in ban telemetry
  - off by default; the client asks once, with a plain explanation of what
    is sent
  - when on, the node reports to the hub: a salted hash of each account
    (never the email), suspended-at when a login returns the ToS suspension
    text, last seen, what the account was doing last (idle, owner trade,
    swap, commons, tutorial walk), node version, Realm build, and account age
    bucket
  - the hub joins this across nodes into the same view scripts/ban-pattern.py
    gave for the fleet, so a wave is visible while it is starting, split by
    activity lane and cohort, and the operator can post a warning or flip
    the minimum version to pause a risky feature
  - telemetry is never used for anything but this and the aggregate is what
    gets published, not per-node data


9. Phasing
----------

  1. Node standalone: vault, deposit/withdraw, /tell login, tutorial walker
     for hand-made accounts, server list refresh, protocol pre-login check
     against the feed, Electron build, signed Windows installer. This is the
     shutdown-notice promise and ships first
  2. Hub v0: hub user accounts, log in from the client, node linking, node
     registry, version feed, current-build endpoint, ban telemetry intake
  3. P2P swaps: offers, rendezvous, receipts, attestations, trade limits
  4. Commons: opt-in items, availability, free capped hand-overs
  4b. Shared vaults: grants with slot quotas, published per-guest contents,
      guest deposit/withdraw via the hub, then guest offers (on-behalf-of
      tag on 6.2, room checks against the guest's slots)
  5. Account supply: separate private service, not part of this document
  6. Later, if ever: an operator-run reserve fleet, modelled as just another
     node with a trusted flag so bringing it back is config, not protocol


10. Open questions
------------------

  - where the hub is hosted (Railway or Vultr)
  - how a player without any alt gets a first account
  - telemetry salt: per node (unlinkable across nodes) or hub-wide (lets the
    hub spot the same account on two nodes)
  - whether receipts should include a packet-level proof (last TRADE packets)
    or just the node's summary
  - clawback or reputation penalty for commons contributors who go dark
  - one character slot per account means no seasonal/non-seasonal switching
    without deletion, which rule 2 forbids; decide per-account seasonality at
    creation and never change it
